---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import coverage from '../data/coverage.json';
import { REPO, RELEASES } from '../lib/site';

const rows = coverage.rows;
const SAPI_LABEL: Record<string, string> = { cli: 'cli-server', fpm: 'php-fpm', apache: 'Apache mod_php' };
const tested = (r: any) => (r.tested as string[]).map((s) => SAPI_LABEL[s]);
const untested = (r: any) => ['cli', 'fpm', 'apache'].filter((s) => !r.tested.includes(s)).map((s) => SAPI_LABEL[s]);
const ranDate = (coverage.generated || '').slice(0, 10);
---
<Layout
  title="Howdah: a defensive firewall for PHP"
  description="Howdah is an open-source, defensive firewall that loads into PHP itself. It blocks abusive multipart upload floods before they exhaust memory, including on end-of-life PHP (5.4 to 8.1) that will not get the official fix."
  path="/"
>
  <div class="hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Open source &middot; defensive &middot; built for PHP 5.4 to 8.5 (see coverage for what is tested)</p>
      <h1>Howdah blocks abusive multipart <em>part/file floods</em> before they exhaust memory.</h1>
      <p class="lede">That is the shape of the PHP2Shell attack. Howdah is a firewall that loads into PHP itself, so it protects even end-of-life PHP (5.4 to 8.1) that will not get the official fix, on the versions tested in the table below.</p>
      <p class="fine">Built for sysadmins who run PHP on their own servers, including servers stuck on old PHP versions. Howdah is defensive only: it recognizes and refuses abusive requests, nothing else.</p>
      <div class="cta">
        <a class="btn primary" href="/install/">Install Howdah</a>
        <a class="btn ghost" href="#coverage">See exactly what has been tested</a>
        <a class="btn ghost" href={REPO}>View on GitHub</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section id="what">
    <div class="wrap cols aside">
      <div>
        <h2>What is Howdah, and why the name?</h2>
        <p>Howdah is a native extension for PHP. When a web request arrives, PHP starts parsing it before any of your code runs. Howdah watches that parsing step and refuses requests that are built to exhaust memory, while PHP is still working through them.</p>
        <p>Version 0.1 does one job: it enforces limits on <code>multipart/form-data</code> uploads, such as the number of parts, the number of files and the length of names. Those limits block by default, even in detect mode. Everything else starts in log-only mode so you can watch it before you let it block.</p>
        <p>It is not a replacement for patching. Upgrade PHP when you can. Use Howdah when you cannot, or until you can.</p>
      </div>
      <aside class="card" aria-labelledby="howdah-word">
        <h3 id="howdah-word">A howdah?</h3>
        <p>A howdah is the covered seat strapped onto an elephant's back: a small armoured shelter that carries someone safely. PHP's mascot is an elephant, so the name fits. Howdah sits on PHP's back and shields what is riding on it.</p>
        <p>Howdah is an independent project. It is not made by, or endorsed by, the PHP project.</p>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="story" class="alt">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>The PHP2Shell story, plainly told</h2>
      <div class="cols two">
        <div>
          <p>On 21 October 2026 the PHP project patched two linked flaws, CVE-2026-109618 and CVE-2026-109619, which security researchers named PHP2Shell. As publicly reported, a crafted <code>multipart/form-data</code> request makes PHP use a lot of memory while it reads the headers of upload parts. A body well under the default 8&nbsp;MB limit can hold hundreds of tiny, empty file parts. Corrupted state from that can, according to the reports, lead to remote code execution without logging in.</p>
          <p>According to public reports, the official fix ships for PHP 8.2 and newer; older branches get no official patch. PHP 8.1 and older still run a very large share of the web.</p>
          <div class="note">
            <h3>What we did and did not verify</h3>
            <p>Our tests reproduce only the harmless <em>structure</em> of such a flood: how many parts and fields, and how large. We did <strong>not</strong> reproduce the CVE's <code>session.upload_progress</code> header-parsing path, so we do <strong>not</strong> claim Howdah blocks CVE-2026-109618 on a stock configuration.</p>
            <p>What we measured: PHP's own <code>max_input_vars</code> and <code>max_file_uploads</code> already keep the flood shape we built small on a default box. On a box where an admin has raised those caps, the same shape used 127 to 173&nbsp;MB, against a 128&nbsp;MB limit, and Howdah's default limits refuse it long before that. Howdah's value is strongest on raised-cap servers and on old PHP with no official fix. See the <a href="/coverage/">coverage table</a> and <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/docs/coverage-data.md`}>the measurements</a>.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 style="margin-top:0">Why only a PHP extension can stop it</h3>
          <ul class="facts">
            <li>PHP reads the whole upload into <code>$_FILES</code> and <code>$_POST</code> before any PHP script runs, so a firewall written in PHP (for example via <code>auto_prepend_file</code>) is too late.</li>
            <li><code>opcache.preload</code> runs once when the server starts, never per request.</li>
            <li>A network firewall sees bytes on the wire, not how PHP will interpret them.</li>
            <li>A compiled extension runs inside PHP's own upload parser, so it can count parts as PHP reads them and stop the request early.</li>
          </ul>
          <p style="margin-top:1rem;font-size:.95rem">The incident details above come from public reports. We are checking them against the official advisories (GHSA-w8hw-w9fx-j79w, GHSA-6wmf-4rv5-p3rp, php.net) and will correct this page if they differ.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="limits">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>What 0.1 enforces</h2>
      <p>These are the default limits. A request that crosses one is refused with HTTP 403 and written to a JSON log. You can change them in a TOML file.</p>
      <div class="tablewrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Default limits (scrolls sideways on narrow screens)">
        <table>
          <caption class="sr-only">Default limits</caption>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Limit</th><th scope="col">Default</th><th scope="col">What it stops</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">max_parts</th><td>2000</td><td>Floods of form-field parts.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">max_files</th><td>200</td><td>Floods of file parts (the PHP2Shell shape).</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">max_filename_bytes</th><td>512</td><td>Oversized filenames.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">max_field_name_bytes</th><td>1024</td><td>Oversized field names.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="legend">These four rules are the exact defense. PHP hands Howdah one event per part with its real name, so every part is counted and every name measured precisely. Two more limits, <code>max_part_header_bytes</code> and <code>max_part_headers</code>, exist but are best-effort: only the <code>Content-Disposition</code> header reaches the extension, so they under-count and back up the four above rather than drive the defense.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="coverage" class="alt">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Coverage: what has actually been tested</h2>
      <p>Every cell below is generated from the test matrix results{ranDate && <> (run {ranDate})</>}. A version says &ldquo;Blocked&rdquo; only if the test for that exact version and server type passed. Versions we have not run are marked &ldquo;Not run&rdquo; and are <strong>not</strong> claimed as protected.</p>
      <div class="tablewrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Coverage by PHP version (scrolls sideways on narrow screens)">
        <table>
          <caption class="sr-only">Coverage of the two flood shapes by PHP version</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">PHP</th>
              <th scope="col">File flood<br /><code>max_files</code></th>
              <th scope="col">Field/part flood<br /><code>max_parts</code></th>
              <th scope="col">Server types tested</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {rows.map((r: any) => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">{r.version ?? r.minor}</th>
                {r.version ? (
                  <>
                    <td><span class="tag ok">Blocked</span></td>
                    <td><span class="tag ok">Blocked</span></td>
                    <td>
                      {tested(r).join(', ')}
                      {untested(r).length > 0 && <small>Not run on: {untested(r).join(', ')}</small>}
                    </td>
                  </>
                ) : (
                  <>
                    <td><span class="tag no">Not run</span></td>
                    <td><span class="tag no">Not run</span></td>
                    <td><small style="margin:0">{r.why}</small></td>
                  </>
                )}
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="legend"><strong>How to read it.</strong> &ldquo;Blocked&rdquo; means a request shaped like the flood got HTTP 403 and a log entry at default limits, on a server configured with raised caps (the exposed setup). The same runs also sent seven ordinary uploads (galleries, settings forms, nested arrays) and none was blocked. Totals: {coverage.totals.pass} checks passed, {coverage.totals.fail} failed, {coverage.totals.skip} skipped. The skips are PHP 5.4 on php-fpm, which the lab could not drive.</p>
      <p class="legend">The table is generated by <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/site/scripts/gen-coverage.mjs`}>a script</a> from <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/lab/integration/matrix-results.tsv`}>the raw results</a>. The full per-server-type table and the honest limits are on the <a href="/coverage/">coverage page</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="install">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Install, verify, roll back</h2>
      <p>Howdah installs from the apt/yum repository or a signed tarball, one build per PHP minor version. The <a href="/install/">install picker</a> gives the exact commands for your OS, PHP source and server type. Start in detect mode, and check the <a href="/coverage/">coverage table</a> for your PHP version first.</p>
      <p><a class="btn primary" href="/install/">Open the install picker</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
